1.1.m:
Seeing that people in the world are short-lived, devoid of knowledge, and behave like beasts,
Vyāsa composed this renowned Purāṇa for their happiness and enlightenment.
Victorious is the Sun, lamp of the world, maker of the universe;
and victorious is Murāri, dark-bodied, armed with the Śārṅga bow;
and victorious is he whose crest is the moon, named Rudra;
and victorious is that luminous god Citrabhānu, the radiant one.
To the mighty king Śatānīka, who was surrounded with royal splendour,
all the great-souled great ṛṣis came to pay a visit:
Bhṛgu, Atri, and Vasiṣṭha, Pulastya, Pulaha, Kratu,
as well as Parāśara, Vyāsa, Sumantu, and Jaimini;
the sage Paila, Yājñavalkya, and Gautama of great ascetic power,
the wise sage Bhāradvāja, as well as Nārada and Parvata;
the great-souled Vaiśampāyana, and Śaunaka of great ascetic power,
Dakṣa, Aṅgiras, Garga, and Gālava of great ascetic power.
Seeing those ṛṣis who had arrived, King Śatānīka,
highly intelligent, approached them and worshipped them according to rule.

Śatānīka said:
“My hands are folded to my head, O Brāhmaṇa, placed at your feet;
tell me the treatise on duty by which I may attain purity.

1.4.m:
If other clans are unavailable, he should avoid each prior one in turn;
and he may roam the whole village if the aforesaid are not found,
excluding outcastes, O mighty-armed one: so said the blessed, omnipresent Lord.

The livelihood of a vrata-observer through alms is regarded as equal to fasting.
At a rite for the gods [he eats] like one bound by a vow, O king, or at a rite for the ancestors like a sage;
when requested, he may eat at will; his vow is not broken.
